# Skill: convert a Bifrost setup into a LiteLLM gateway config

You are helping the user move from Bifrost to a LiteLLM gateway. You read their Bifrost
`config.json` and write a LiteLLM `config.yaml`, plus the commands that recreate their teams,
customers, and virtual keys in LiteLLM with the same `sk-bf-*` key values. The goal is that
clients keep their `provider/model` strings and their keys, and change only the base URL.

Reference documentation: https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/migrate/bifrost

Never invent a credential, a model, a budget, or a limit. Provider keys are always referenced
as `os.environ/NAME`, never written into the file. Batch questions into as few messages as you
can, and skip anything the user already told you.

## 1. Collect the Bifrost config

Ask the user for their Bifrost `config.json` (by default in the Bifrost app directory, or the
file passed with `-app-dir`). If Bifrost was configured through its web UI and stores config
in its database, ask them to export it or to paste the relevant sections; the Bifrost HTTP API
redacts virtual key values, so it cannot be used to copy keys.

Also search their application code for the Bifrost base URL (`:8080`, `/openai`, `/anthropic`,
`/genai`, `/litellm`), the `x-bf-vk` header, and the model strings each app sends. Those
strings decide the `model_name` values below.

## 2. Inventory providers and credentials

List every provider under `providers` and every key under `providers.<name>.keys[]`. Show the
user a table with one row per key: provider, key `name`, the environment variable its `value`
references (`env.OPENAI_KEY_1` becomes `os.environ/OPENAI_KEY_1`), `models`, and `weight`.
If a provider key's `value` is a literal secret rather than `env.VAR`, do not copy it into
`config.yaml`; propose a variable name and ask the user to put the secret there. (Virtual key
values are different: see step 6.) `vault.<path>` values need a LiteLLM secret manager,
which is an Enterprise feature; ask how they want to handle them.

For Azure keys also carry over `azure_key_config.endpoint`; for Bedrock, `region` and how the
gateway gets AWS credentials (`access_key`/`secret_key` env vars, or `role_arn`); for Vertex,
`project_id`, `region`, and `auth_credentials`.

## 3. Convert providers

| Bifrost | LiteLLM |
|---|---|
| A key with `models: ["*"]` | A wildcard deployment: `model_name: "<provider>/*"`, `litellm_params.model: "<provider>/*"` |
| Several keys on one provider with `weight` | One deployment per key, all with the same `model_name`, each with `weight` in `litellm_params` |
| A key with a specific `models` list | One deployment per model: `model_name: "<provider>/<model>"`, `model: "<provider>/<model>"` |
| key `aliases` (`"gpt-4o": "my-deployment"`) | `model_name: "<provider>/gpt-4o"`, `model: "<provider>/my-deployment"` |
| `env.VAR` | `os.environ/VAR` |
| `network_config.base_url` | `api_base` |
| `network_config.extra_headers` | `extra_headers` in `litellm_params` |
| `network_config.default_request_timeout_in_seconds` | `router_settings.timeout`, or `timeout` on one deployment if providers differ |
| `network_config.max_retries` | `router_settings.num_retries`, or `num_retries` on one deployment |
| `azure_key_config.endpoint` | `api_base` on an `azure/<deployment>` model; ask for `api_version` |
| `bedrock_key_config` (`region`, `access_key`, `secret_key`, `role_arn`) | `aws_region_name`, `aws_access_key_id`, `aws_secret_access_key`, `aws_role_name` |
| `vertex_key_config` (`project_id`, `region`, `auth_credentials`) | `vertex_project`, `vertex_location`, `vertex_credentials` |
| `custom_provider_config.base_provider_type: openai` | `model: "openai/<model>"` with `api_base` set to the custom provider's `base_url` |
| `concurrency_and_buffer_size` | Usually drop it; `max_parallel_requests` on a deployment is the closest LiteLLM setting |

Keep the Bifrost model naming (`openai/gpt-4o-mini`) so no client changes its model strings.

An exact `model_name` always takes priority over a wildcard. Once you add a deployment named
`openai/gpt-4o-mini`, the `openai/*` deployments no longer serve that model. So when one key
lists specific models and other keys on the same provider allow `*`, add a deployment for
each of those models on every key that served it in Bifrost, with the same weights, or that
model loses its other keys. Show the user which deployments you repeated and why.

When `network_config` is set on only some providers, put `timeout` and `num_retries` on those
providers' deployments rather than in `router_settings`, so other providers keep the defaults.

`use_for_batch_api` has no LiteLLM equivalent; that key serves normal traffic too. Say so.
When a key uses a `regex:` model pattern, tell the user LiteLLM wildcards only match `*`, and
list the models explicitly or fall back to a `provider/*` wildcard with their agreement.

## 4. Convert governance, routing, and plugins

| Bifrost | LiteLLM |
|---|---|
| `routing_rules[]` with `cel_expression: "true"` and `fallbacks` | `litellm_settings.default_fallbacks: [...]` |
| A routing rule whose `targets` name one model and has `fallbacks` | `router_settings.fallbacks: [{"<provider>/<model>": ["<fallback>", ...]}]` |
| A rule matching on the model (`model == 'gpt-4o'`, `model.startsWith('gpt-4')`) that targets another provider | Under each matching `model_name`, a deployment on the target provider with `order: 1`, and the original provider's deployments with `order: 2`; LiteLLM tries `order: 2` only when `order: 1` fails, so the `order: 2` deployments replace that rule's `fallbacks` and need no `router_settings.fallbacks` entry. Weights still split traffic within one order level. A prefix match cannot be expressed, so ask which models it should cover and list them. Keep `azure/<model>` names too only if clients send them |
| Provider-only `fallbacks` (`"openai"`, `"anthropic/"`) | LiteLLM fallbacks are model names, so ask which model each provider fallback should use and write it as `<provider>/<model>` |
| A routing rule with a CEL expression on headers or metadata | Tag routing: deployments with `tags: [...]`, `router_settings.enable_tag_filtering: true`; callers send `x-litellm-tags`. Show the user each rule and the tag you chose |
| `scope: team` / `virtual_key` routing rules | No direct equivalent: `models` on a team or key only limits access, it does not route. Give that team or key its own model names (for example `premium/gpt-4o`) whose deployments follow the rule's targets, or keep the key's existing access and tell the user the rule is gone |
| CEL on runtime state (`budget_used`, `request_type`, token counts) | No equivalent; drop the rule and tell the user. For `request_type == 'embedding'`, clients name the embedding model directly. LiteLLM's [budget fallbacks](https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/budget_fallbacks) cover some budget-based switching |
| Rule `priority` and overlapping rules | No equivalent; when two rules match the same model, ask which one should win |
| `plugins: semantic_cache` (`ttl`, `threshold`, `embedding_model`) | `litellm_settings.cache: true`, `cache_params: {type: redis-semantic, ttl, similarity_threshold, redis_semantic_cache_embedding_model, host: os.environ/REDIS_HOST, port: os.environ/REDIS_PORT, password: os.environ/REDIS_PASSWORD}`. The embedding model must be a `model_name` the gateway serves, so add a deployment for it. Redis needs the RediSearch module (Redis Stack or Redis 8). `dimension`, `cache_by_model`, and `cache_by_provider` have no equivalent; say so |
| `plugins: otel` (`collector_url`, `service_name`) | `litellm_settings.callbacks: ["otel"]`, with `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` set to the collector URL and `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` to the service name, in the gateway's environment |
| `plugins: datadog` | `litellm_settings.callbacks: ["datadog"]` with `DD_API_KEY` and `DD_SITE` |
| `plugins: maxim`, `logging`, others | Ask which tool they log to and use the matching callback from https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/observability/callbacks |
| `mcp.client_configs[]` (`name`, `connection_type`, `connection_string`, `stdio_config`) | `mcp_servers: {<name>: {url: ..., transport: http or sse}}`, or `transport: stdio` with `command` and `args`; stdio needs `LITELLM_ENABLE_MCP_STDIO=true` |
| `config_store: postgres` or `sqlite` | `general_settings.database_url: os.environ/DATABASE_URL` (LiteLLM needs Postgres; SQLite is not supported). If `config_store` is enabled, settings edited in the Bifrost UI live in that database and may be missing from `config.json`; ask the user to export them or confirm the file is current |
| `encryption_key` | `LITELLM_SALT_KEY`, a new value; it encrypts credentials LiteLLM stores, and Bifrost's key cannot be reused |
| `client.enforce_auth_on_inference: false` | No equivalent: LiteLLM always requires a key on inference routes, so every client needs one (step 6) |
| `client.allowed_origins`, `drop_excess_requests`, `retry_backoff_initial`, `retry_backoff_max` | Usually drop them and say so |

Always add:

```yaml
general_settings:
  master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY
  database_url: os.environ/DATABASE_URL
```

## 5. Show the result and ask before writing

Show the user the full `config.yaml` and, under it, a short table mapping each Bifrost
provider key and routing rule to the LiteLLM deployments or settings that replace it. Call out
anything you dropped or changed in meaning. Write the file only after they confirm, and do not
overwrite an existing `config.yaml` without asking.

## 6. Teams, customers, and virtual keys

Read `governance.teams[]`, `customers[]`, `virtual_keys[]`, `budgets[]`, and `rate_limits[]`.
A budget is linked to its owner by `virtual_key_id`, `team_id`, or `customer_id` on the budget
(or by `budget_id` on older configs), and a rate limit by `rate_limit_id` on the owner.

Convert durations: `1M` becomes `1mo`, `1w` becomes `7d`, `1Q` becomes `90d`, `1Y` becomes
`365d`; `30s`, `5m`, `1h`, and `1d` stay the same. `max_limit` becomes `max_budget`,
`request_max_limit` becomes `rpm_limit` and `token_max_limit` becomes `tpm_limit`, but only
when the matching reset duration is `1m`; otherwise tell the user LiteLLM limits are per minute
and ask what value to use. Keys, teams, and customers all accept `rpm_limit` and `tpm_limit`.

A team's `customer_id` has no LiteLLM equivalent: a customer's budget does not cover a team's
spend. Create both and tell the user the link is gone.

A virtual key's `provider_configs[].allowed_models` becomes the key's `models` list, written in
`provider/model` form (`["openai/*"]` when it allows a whole provider). `is_active: false`
becomes `"blocked": true`; ask whether inactive keys should be migrated at all.

Write the commands as one script in this order, capturing each `team_id` from the
`team/new` response (for example with `jq -r .team_id`) and passing it to that team's keys.
Do not run it against a gateway unless the user asks:

```bash
curl -X POST $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/team/new \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"team_alias": "<team name>", "max_budget": <usd>, "budget_duration": "<duration>"}'

curl -X POST $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/customer/new \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"user_id": "<customer id>", "alias": "<customer name>", "max_budget": <usd>, "budget_duration": "<duration>",
       "rpm_limit": <n>, "tpm_limit": <n>}'

curl -X POST $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/key/generate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"key": "<sk-bf-... value>", "key_alias": "<virtual key name>", "team_id": "<team_id from team/new>",
       "models": ["<provider>/<model or *>"], "max_budget": <usd>, "budget_duration": "<duration>",
       "rpm_limit": <n>, "tpm_limit": <n>}'
```

`key` must be the existing `sk-bf-*` value so clients keep working. If a virtual key's
`value` is `env.VAR`, reference `$VAR` in the command rather than printing the secret. If it is
a literal `sk-bf-*` value in the config, it is a client credential the user already shares
with their apps, so it can go in the command; tell them the script now contains live keys. Omit any field the user's config does not set.

## 7. Start and verify

If the user does not already run LiteLLM, give them the command, with one `-e` per variable
you named:

```bash
docker run -d -p 4000:4000 -v $(pwd)/config.yaml:/app/config.yaml \
  -e LITELLM_MASTER_KEY -e LITELLM_SALT_KEY -e DATABASE_URL \
  -e <PROVIDER_KEY_VARS> \
  docker.litellm.ai/berriai/litellm:latest --config /app/config.yaml
```

Tell them `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` and `LITELLM_SALT_KEY` must be set first (for example `sk-`
followed by `openssl rand -hex 32`), and that the salt key must never change once credentials
are stored. A hosted 1-click deploy (Railway or Render) is linked from
https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/migrate/bifrost if they would rather not run Docker. For more than
one replica, add `redis_host`, `redis_port`, and `redis_password` under `router_settings`.

Once it is up, check `curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/health/readiness`, then send one request per
model string the apps use, with one of the imported `sk-bf-*` keys:

```bash
curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <sk-bf-... key>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"model": "<provider/model the app sends>", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "ping"}]}'
```

Show the user each response and the `x-litellm-model-group` and
`x-litellm-overhead-duration-ms` response headers. Report any string that fails rather than
renaming it.

## 8. Hand back

Tell the user what each client changes: the base URL becomes the LiteLLM gateway URL (`/openai`
and `/anthropic` both become the root URL, `/genai` becomes `/gemini`), and clients that send
the key only in `x-bf-vk` must send it as `Authorization: Bearer` instead. Model strings and key
values stay the same, and so do request-body `fallbacks` written as `provider/model`;
provider-only entries such as `"openai"` must become full model names. Point them at
https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/migrate/bifrost#feature-mapping for anything not covered here.
